There is a way to do this without calculus, though it might be marginally harder. Drop a perpendicular from the top of one circle to touch the ellipse, intersecting the $x$-axis at the centre of the circle. The intersection of the circle with the ellipse forms the point of tangency, so through this point we can draw a tangent to the ellipse and one to the circle. The other tangent to the ellipse makes a $45^{\circ}$ angle with the $x$-axis. Find the point of intersection of these tangents, and look for symmetry somewhere. The equation $\frac{x^2}{a^2}+ \frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1$ for the ellipse produces the slope $-\frac{b^2}{a^2}$ (which makes an angle with the $x$-axis of $\tan (\abs{\arctan(-b/a)})$ with the ellipse and thus of $\arctan(-b/a)$ with the tangent to the arc of the ellipse, as the slope of the tangent line corresponds to the tangent of the angle of inclination of the tangent.) The same slope appears at the point of tangency of the other side of the ellipse. Both tangents to the ellipse pass through $(r,0)$, which means they both satisfy the equation of the tangent  to the ellipse at the point of tangency, and the graph of this tangent will produce the ellipse between the two tangents and thus include them in its upper part. The symmetric tangent to the circle will cross the intersection of the tangents to the ellipse and will intersect the circle at the point of tangency of the circle. Or, cut, dig, hunt.
